Biker reacts lightning fast and prevents worse
On Friday evening, a traffic accident occurred on the country road between Machtolsheim and Merklingen, in which a motorcyclist was only slightly injured thanks to his quick reaction.
Driver overlooks bikers
A 44-year-old motorist wanted to turn around 21.30 with her VW coming from Merklingen to the left on the state road 1230 in the direction of Machtolsheim.
She overlooked the 24-year-old motorcyclist coming from Nellingen.
In the collision, hand and foot brake levers fly away
The biker was still trying to dodge the car, still grazing it at the rear vehicle area.
As a result of the grazing process, the hand and foot brake levers were torn off on the Suzuki and the driver was no longer able to brake.
Present-of-mind, he steered his motorcycle into the green stiffs next to the road and had it rolled out without falling.
Biker suffers leg injury
Due to the collision with the car, the biker nevertheless suffered an injury and had to be treated in a clinic.
Mamming: Corona mass infection: 174 harvest workers infected
After a corona mass infection on a large vegetable farm in Mamming in Lower Bavaria, almost 500 people are quarantined and are no longer allowed to leave the company.
A security service monitors the nightlife ban.
A total of 174 harvest helpers tested positive for the corona pathogen Sars-CoV-2 during the series examination, as the Landratsamt Dingolfing-Landau announced on Saturday evening.
"In order to avoid a further spread, we unfortunately have to take this step to protect the population," explained Landrat Werner Bumeder (CSU).
Previously, seven employees had already been infected in the company - this was the occasion of the series test.
The farm is a large vegetable farm that grows strawberries, cucumbers, cabbage and beetroot - the cucumbers, however, only for inlay jars.
Mamming is a rural municipality with more than 3300 inhabitants not far from Dingolfing with its large BMW plant.
Bumeder appealed to the citizens to keep calm: "According to our information, it is a closed circle of people".
Infected people were separated from uninfected employees in the yard, the site is now surrounded by a shut-off fence.
Greens and SPD gave the state government co-responsibility.
The two opposition factions accused Prime Minister Markus Söder (CSU) and his cabinet of neglecting the control of accommodations of seasonal workers.
"This can take bitter revenge if such local infection events have to reach out and regional lockdowns have to be ordered," said Jürgen Mistol, the Greens' parliamentary managing director.
The SPD sees this very similarly: "After the corona outbreak in the Wiesenhof slaughterhouse in mid-May, we have already demanded a special control program for farms with harvest helpers," said environmental expert Florian von Brunn.
“Especially the hygiene conditions in the workplace and the living conditions must be sharply controlled from our point of view.
This obviously didn’t happen.”
The eruption had come "with announcement."
Aidenbachstraße underground station: Dangerous dispute: 19-year-old falls into the track bed after push
In a dispute between two youth groups at Aidenbachstraße underground station, a 19-year-old fell through a nudge and slipped into the track bed.
Obersendling - On Friday, there was a dispute between two youth groups at around 16:15.
A passer-by alerted the police, who approached with several strips.
According to initial investigations, a Munich (17) pushed a 19-year-old to the ground.
In the process, the young man slipped over the edge of the platform and fell into the track bed.
People from the 17-year-old's group helped the plunger climb out of the track.
There was no danger from an incoming railway.
According to the police, there were clashes between the two persons.
The 17-year-old initially fled from the crime scene, but his personal details could be determined afterwards.
Accompanied by his father, he went to police inspection 29.
The 17-year-old is being investigated for assault.
Report: Seehofer pushes for longer data retention
Data retention is currently suspended.
According to a media report, however, Federal Interior Minister Horst Seehofer calls for an extension of the storage period.
He wants to strengthen the fight against child abuse.
According to a media report, German Interior Minister Horst Seehofer (CSU) is calling for extended data retention - instead of the planned ten weeks of half a year.
As the "Bild am Sonntag" reports, Seehofer Justice Minister Christine Lambrecht (SPD) asked in a letter for a quick change in the law.
Seehofer thus wants to strengthen the measures against child abuse.
"I think it makes sense to make urgently necessary adjustments in the law already now," the newspaper quotes from the letter.
Data retention is currently suspended.
A decision by the ECJ is expected on this.
Seehofer, however, wants the changes beforehand, so that they could be applied immediately after a possible positive verdict, the report said.
A spokesman for the Justice Department told the newspaper that it could only be meaningfully considered whether legal adjustments were needed if the courts had ruled.
In the fight against child abuse and child pornography, Seehofer, together with the interior ministers of the countries, had already advocated data retention in June.
NBNA: Strip-club trouble for Clippers star Lou Williams
Excitement in the NBA!
Lou Williams (33), basketball pro of the Los Angeles Clippers, is said to have left the tournament quarter at Disney World in Orlando/Florida with permission for a funeral.
According to U.S. media reports, however, he visited the strip club "Magic City" in Atlanta in the evening.
Williams, who is said to have admitted the visit, is back in isolation, tested daily for Covid-19.
His reason for the visit: Chicken Wings.
In February last year "Sweet Lou," as he's called in the NBA, has already voiced his love for exactly these wings.
“Magic City and Blue Flame (another strip club in Atlanta) have the best wings in the city.
NBA insider Shams Charania announced on Twitter on Sunday that the NBA Williams has mandated a ten-day quarantine.
With this, the Clippers star misses the first two official games after the Test match phase.
At Disney World, the NBA wants to play the Corona season.
Currently, the best 22 teams with training games are preparing for the season finale, which begins in the night on Friday.
Söder expresses himself after mass infection on corona situation
Health Minister Melanie Huml (CSU) has already announced on Sunday a te-substantive for large farms.
The press conference will be shown live on our homepage.
The case in Mamming in Dingolfing-Landau district has startled authorities and politics.
Nearly 500 harvest helpers from several countries are currently working on the large vegetable farm, and over a third has become infected with the corona pathogen.
The farm is now under quarantine and is cordoned off with a construction fence.
No one is allowed to leave the premises, food is delivered.
Authorities now offer free corona tests to the municipality's more than 3300 residents.
Since the harvest workers had little contact with the local population, the district office so far assumes that the infections have not spread beyond the farm.
'Conscientious bites': Confession of the alleged arsonist of Nantes
"My client has showned himself cooperatively," lawyer Quentin Chabert told the newspaper.
The defender added: "He very much regrets the deeds, the confession was a liberation for him."
“His mandate is plagued by remorse and taken away by the extent of the destruction.”
In the event of a conviction, according to the prosecutor's office, the man faces ten years imprisonment and a fine of 150,000 euros.
The Gothic Saint-Peter-and-Paul-Cathedral in Nantes had been badly damaged in a fire during the night on Saturday last week.
The fire had broken out in three places far apart in the church.
Laboratory tests confirmed suspicions of arson.
The now detained suspect had already been arrested a few hours after the fire.
He was released after one day.
The man works as a volunteer for the diocese of Nantes and was responsible for the closure of the cathedral on the eve of the fire.
Since there was no trace of a violent intrusion into the church, he quickly fell into the sight of the investigators.
Discuss abolition of the 50+1 rule
Former DFB President Reinhard Grindel has recommended thinking about abolishing the 50+1 rule.
"German football should at least discuss setting conditions and clear rules for investors before the EU Commission creates completed facts," Grindel told "Bild am Sonntag."
He emphasized: "For the clubs, which are most likely to become Bayern and Dortmund, the 50+1 rules do not work anyway: see Leipzig or Leverkusen.
In this respect, it is not possible to avoid the debate whether it is not necessary to facilitate the involvement of associations that want to break into this phalanx.”
However, he has doubts as to whether there is a willingness in the German Football League to allocate majority shareholdings, which are prohibited in the Bundesliga by the 50+1 rule.
"It remains a legal risk to draw investment fences in a European single market around the business location Bundesliga," Grindel said.
The former DFB boss sees the danger that "the attempts to ensure more competitive balance in the Bundesliga, in the end only weaken the competitiveness of our top clubs at European level, but do not lead to more tension in the championship."
Even an equal distribution of TV money will not have a decisive effect.
“FC Bayern makes a turnover of €750 million, Borussia Dortmund from €500 million and RB Leipzig of €300 million.
So the differences are already immense among the first three clubs alone.
The other 15 clubs have an average household of 130 million," the 58-year-old explained.
Looking at the past, Grindel feels betrayed.
"Unfortunately, there have been individuals who noticed this mistake and stabbed through to the press, rather than address me," Grindel said about accepting an expensive watch that had led to his resignation in April 2019.
"That's really bothered me humanely."
He had received the gift worth around 11,000 euros from a Ukrainian functionary.
"I committed a compliance violation, and it was right to draw the consequences from that," Grindel said.
"With the ominous watch gift, I have been completely transparent and unsuspecting.
Many in the DFB knew about it.”
He had never "made a secret from the gift," the 58-year-old assured.
“This may be called naïve, in any case, this form of transparency was the complete opposite of corruption.”
"Black Lives Matter": Injured and a dead
Protests against police violence in the United States have escalated again through Donald Trump's use of federal police officers in several cities.
On Saturday, clashes between protesters and polices occurred in various cities.
Three people were injured in Louisville and 16 people were arrested in Seattle.
In Austin, one person was killed by gunfire while a demonstration train just crossed an intersection.
Previously, clashes occurred again in Portland during protests against racism and police violence.
The security forces, including officials of the federal police, used tear gas during the night on Saturday and forcibly drove protesters apart.
Hundreds, partly armed protesters marched through Louisville in Kentucky on Saturday, demanding that those responsible for Breonna Taylor's death should be helded to account.
The 26-year-old student had been shooted in March during a drug raid at her Louisville apartment.
The target of the raid was someone else who didn't live there at all, and no drugs were found either.
When on Saturday, a member of a group calling itself an armed alliance of black Americans fired a shot, three people were injured.
According to NBC, they were taken to the hospital.
None of them were in danger.
The police set up barricades because counter-demonstrators of a white militia had also formed.
Ultimately, there was no encounter between the two groups.
In Austin, Texas, one person was shot and died during a "Black Lives Matter" demonstration.
Police took a suspect into custody, according to their own accounts.
She did not yet provide further details on the circumstances of the crime.
Mayor Steve Adler said he, like many in the city, was "inconsolable and stunned" because of what had happened, according to the New York Times.
Also in the American metropolis of Seattle, protesters delivered clashes with the police on Saturday.
They set fire to construction trucks in front of a juvenile prison, the police used glare grenades and pepper spray.
According to the Seattle Times, 16 people were arrested for resisting police officers or assault.
A courthouse has been set on fire in the town of Oakland, California.
A small group of protesters smashed windows, fired firecrackers and blinded police officers with lasers, Oakland police said late on Saturday evening (local time).
The fire was quickly extinguished.
In Aurora, a suburb of Denver in Colorado, a car drove through a crouple that demonstrated on a highway.
As the police announced, one of the protesters fired a gun and at least one person was taken to the hospital injured.
In addition, windows were smashed there in a court and a fire was set, as the police announced.
Investigations have been commenced.
Rakoczy in Bad Kissingen: Historical costumes defy Corona
Although the big snack was missing in the spa town, visitors and performers enjoyed the Rakoczy flair of the alternative program.
It was unfamiliar for visitors, performers and organizers: the Rakoczy weekend without Rakoczy feast.
But: Even if the big party mile in the city center was missing, the crowds and program highlights such as Saale, Rakoczy-Ball or Festzug, many got their money's worth with the Corona-compliant alternative program.
"I think we've managed to spread Rakoczy flair," says spa director Sylvie Thormann.
The guests would have received it well.
In principle, one returns to the beginnings of the Rakoczy festival, when only the historical was the focus of the festival.
Pleasant Atmosphere
Vesna Konhäuser has always been attending Rakoczy-Fest Bad Kissingen with her husband for more than 25 years.
So again this time in the Corona year.
The couple have made themselves comfortable at the Luitpold-Casino, observe the historical ones while strolling and listen to the Cuban live music at the casino restaurant.
"I also find it quite pleasant," says Konhäuser.
She doesn't miss the crowds that otherwise belong to Rakoczy.
However, she lacks the usual program highlights, especially Saale burns on Friday evening as well as the historic procession and the closing fireworks on Sunday evening.
"That's a pity.
The weather would have been great this year," she says.
Rakoczy equestrian tournament to take place again
For the performers of the historical spa guests, the alternative program sometimes meant more work.
For example, Christina Gold, who participates in a normal year at the procession and can be seen there on the carriage of the Kurgartencafé.
This year on Saturdays and Sundays she was walking in the city.
"It's more exhausting, otherwise we'll have it easier," she says.
The equestrian club had less to do, even if individual riders were travelling in the park.
"We very much hope that the equestrian quadrille will be allowed to take place again next year," says chairwoman Ulrike Waldhofen.
She announced that after several years of pause in 2021, a Rakoczy equestrian tournament would also take place again.
28 corona cases at Spanish second division team Fuenlabrada
The Spanish football second division CF Fuenlabrada slides deeper and deeper into the crisis because of a corona outbreak.
Since the newcomer from near Madrid had flowned to the city of A Coruña for a match on July 20 despite the first Corona cases, one low blow has followed the next.
By Sunday, 28 players or team members had tested positive, one came to the hospital, as the club announced.
Fuenlabrada from the city of the same namery south-west of Madrid still had the chance to reach the promotion playoffs.
Now even threaten relegation to the B group of the Second Division.
Last Monday's match against Deportivo La Coruña was cancelled at the last moment, prosecutors are investigating for a possible offence against public health, the top sports authority CSD is considering the club's forced relegation.
The players as well as other team members are in quarantine in a hotel.
"Until the sixth day of quarantine, they didn't receive a fresh towel and no gel," the sports newspaper "AS" wrote.
In photos you can see how players hung underwear and club clothes in their rooms in the hotel to dry, after they apparently had to wash them themselves.
Also, the bedding had not been changed for five days.
Another problem is the food.
It is neither good nor sufficient, wrote "AS".
The club management, on the other hand, said they were satisfied with the hotel.
Javier Tebas Llanas, the club's lawyer dismissented the allegation of intentionally jeopardizing public health.
They have not violated the Corona rules established by the Football Association, emphasized the son of the President of the Spanish Primera Division, Javier Tebas, as the news Agency Europa Press and the sports newspaper "Marca" reported.
Before departure, there had been four infected persons by July 19, all of whom had been isolated and did not travel along.
Directly prior to Monday's flight to A Coruña, all players and team members had been retested.
When the team was already in A Coruña, "five or six" other team members had the Corona test positive.
The game was canceled 60 minutes before kick-off, Tebas Llanas said.
Police have to capture ripped-out horses at Dampfach
Two animals were travelling on the road.
The police led them back to their owner.
On Sunday morning shortly before 6 a.m., a road user shared two free-roaming horses on the feeder between Donnersdorf/Steinsfeld junction and Theres motorway junction.
At the arrival of the officials, the horses were already again between the feeder and the village of Dampfach.
Shortly before Dampfach by the officials the trust of the horses could be gained, they each had a rope carried in the patrol carfolded.
Cyclist Tony Martin: career continuation imaginable
Time trial specialist Tony Martin is thinking about a continuation of his career.
Frankfurt/Main - The seven-time time trial world champion Tony Martin may well imagine continuing his cycling career.
"I'm currently sticking out with the team the general conditions of how and in what form it could continue," the 35-year-old told the "Frankfurter Allgemeine Sonntagszeitung."
Martin is currently employed by the Dutch team Jumbo-Visma and had considered ending his career after this year.
Negotiations with his team were also about the number of race days, he said.
Or about other problems that would plague him as a family man, Martin said.
He used to get up after a fall, and the first thought was: "Quick back on the wheel.
Today I think as the father of a little daughter: What if.”
The professional cyclist acknowledged that there were certainly aspects with which he finds himself increasingly difficult.
“But it was good for me to realize what I have about cycling based on the Corona period and how much fun it is for me.”
The time trial specialist is getting back into competition at the Tour de l'Ain (5 to 9 August).
Baven: At least four dead on Bayern's roads at the weekend
The beautiful weather attracts bikers to the streets in many places.
For at least three people, the tour by motorcycle ends fatally.
An 18-year-old pedestrian also dies.
Schönthal - In accidents on Bavaria's roads, several, mostly young people have died over the weekend.
An 18-year-old motorcyclist was captured on Saturday near Schönthal (Cham district) by a car that came to the opposite lane.
Why the car fell into the opposite lane was initially unclear.
In Upper Bavaria on Saturday, a 22-year-old collided with a horse-drawn carriage on his machine.
He was travelling downhill at Beilngries, according to police.
In a left turn, he bounced against the carriage.
His injuries were so severe that he died at the scene of the accident.
Of the twelve occupants of the carriage, a man was severely injured and a man slightly injured, the police said.
An 18-year-old died in another fatal accident in Bad Kötzting, Upper Palatinate.
He had put himself on a road during the night to Sunday and was rolled over by a 42-year-old motorist.
According to the police, the 18-year-old wanted to sleep.
The motorist could no longer brake in time.
At Schönberg (Freyung-Grafenau district), a 57-year-old biker bounced head-on against a car in a right turn despite full braking on Saturday.
He died at the scene of the accident.
The 57-year-old driver suffered a shock, the remaining three people in the car were uninjured.
The safety of motorcyclists was addressed on Saturday by Bavaria's Transport Minister Kerstin Schreyer (CSU).
In a letter to the organizer of the biker protests against driving bans announced for August, Gabor Kovacs, the minister referred to extensive investments such as underrun protection on guardrails and the mitigation of dangerous curves.
"For the safety of motorcyclists, we have achieved a lot in recent years," the letter said.
“The number of motorized two-cyclists killed decreased by around 28 percent compared to 2011.”
Kovac test with Monaco against Frankfurt
Frankfurt/Main (dpa) - For football coach Niko Kovac with his new club AS Monaco, there will soon be a reunion with Eintracht Frankfurt.
The eight-time French champion performs at the Hesse, the Croatian's former club, on 1 August (18:00).
For the Bundeslist, it is the first and only tough test before the return match in the Europa League against FC Basel (6 August).
Due to the coronavirus pandemic, the encounter will be held without spectators.
“We’re especially looking forward to this game.
It's always nice to come to Frankfurt and meet friends," Kovac explained.
Sportingly, this is an interesting test, said the 48-year-old.
Prior to his move to FC Bayern Munich, Kovac had coached the Hessians from 2016 to 2018 and led to victory in the DFB Cup.
"A top-class opponent like Monaco is a good test for us with regard to the match against Basel.
Like all from Frankfurt, I am looking forward to the reunion with Niko Kovac," Eintracht coach Adi Hütter said.
The 50-year-old Austrian had succeeded the Croatian at Eintracht.
In March, the Frankfurt had lost the quarter-final first leg against Basel in their own stadium with 0:3.
Almost 50 new infections: Hof district narrowly past lockdown
In the Hof district, the number of well-known corona new infections at the weekend has increased further: from 39 on Friday and 43 on Saturday (one person has recovered and no longer falls into this statistic) to 47 on Sunday.
In addition, there is a case in the city yard.
Thus, the Raum Hof is approaching the critical mark of 50 new infections per 100,000 inhabitants in one week.
700 killed by monsoon rains in Southeast Asia
More than 9 million people are affected by the severe flooding in Southeast Asia, mainly in Bangladesh, India and Nepal.
Due to the heavy monsoon rain, a total of 700 people were killed.
In Bangladesh, the Red Crescent in boats strives to reach the isolated people.
Entire villages are under water.
Farmers have lost their crops.
Tank rhinos in national park die from landslides
In Kaziranga National Park, several of the 120 tank rhinos died from landslides.
They are under protection and those considered to be at risk of extinction.
This year, the floodwaters are particularly bad about what scientists attribute to climate change.
According to the United Nations, Bangladesh is one of the countries most affected by global warming.
New apartments are empty
In the town of Cox's Bazaar in southern Bangladesh, the state has built apartments for 650 displaced by the floodwaters.
So far, only a few have moved in.
It's hard to get people to Cox's bazaar.
Enthusiasm is also within reach.
If you used to have a house, it was still so small and resembled a hut, often does not want to spend your life in a large apartment block.
Many apartments are empty, but - as the government wants - should soon be sourced from those who have lost their house due to the floods.
60 new corona infections in just one day in Vienna
In a 24-hour comparison, 60 new corona infections were reported in Vienna - in Lower Austria there were 22 new infections.
The State Medical Directorate of Vienna and the medical crisis team of the City of Vienna regularly inform about the current case figures and further key figures on the Covid-19 virus: As of Sunday, July 26, 8.00 a.m., 4,840 (+60) positive tests have been confirmed in Vienna.
The number of deaths associated with the virus remains 204.
4,090 (+48) people are recovering.
22 new coronavirus infections in Lower Austria
The number of coronavirus infections in Lower Austria rose on Sunday by 22 to 3,288.
One sufferer has recovered, according to the medical staff.
Thus, the number of recoveries increases to 2,936.
Currently suffering from Covid-19, 247 patients were in the state.
To date, 147,253 tests have been carried out.
105 people died in connection with the lung disease.
Argentina rejects counter-offer from creditors
The new demands could not be met, the Ministry of Economic Affairs in Buenos Aires announced on Saturday.
"This would not only be irresponsible, but also unfair," it said in a statement.
“While 50 percent of children in Argentina live in poverty, we cannot increase the short-term gains of our creditors.”
The debt of the second-largest economy in South America is no longer viable under current conditions.
That's why Argentina is asking its private creditors to forego part of their claims of around €66 billion (around €59 billion).
If there is no agreement, the country threatens the state bankruptcy again.
It would be the ninth default in Argentinian history.
Most recently, lenders had demanded higher interest rates on government bonds and changes to some contractual clauses.
However, the Argentinian government is sticking to its last offer and is not prepared to make any further concessions.
Argentina is undergoing a serious financial and economic crisis.
The rate of inflation was more than 50 percent recently.
For the current year, experts are expectinging economic strength to fall by around ten percent.
At the end of May, Argentina had not settled $503 million interest receivables and had slipped into a limiteded default.
Professionally arrived - not yet private
"It's doing super well" - Dr. Stefanie Kramer draws this conclusion after her first days at the notary's office in Eggenfelden.
In the end, everything happened very quickly: In mid-May, Dr. Stefanie Kramer learned that she will succeed Lutz Feller at the Eggenfelden notary office.
Already six weeks later, at the beginning of Julue, the doctoral lawyer took up the work, the normal day-to-day business has long been in full swing.
"However, I still have to arrive privately," she explains in conversation with the Rottaler Anzeiger and laughs.
Of course, the short lead time was not enough to relocate the food center from Regensburg completely to Eggenfelden, reports the Dingolfingerin native.
She is currently looking for a suitable apartment or a house in the city with her husband, who works as a judge at the district court in Regensburg.
At least her husband was able to apply for short-term parental leave in order to be able to ensure the care of the two daughters (31⁄2 and 11⁄2 years old).
In order not to have to commute from Regensburg every day, she also has an overnight accommodation with her parents in Dingolfing, explains the 39-year-old.
Growing up in the neighbouring district, the city of Eggenfelden has of course long become known to it.
During visits on site with her family in the past months, she again stated: "Eggenfelden is a really nice small town".
She was all the more pleased that she did the race in the selection process for the vacancy.
Her native Lower Bavaria also plays a central role in Dr. Stefanie Kramer’s professional career: After studying law in Passau, she completed her clerkship studies and the 2nd state examination in Regensburg.
After several positions as a nota-assessor, among others in Rehau and Hof in Oberfranken, Babenhausen im Unterallgäu and Regensburg - during this time she also shoulded "in addition" the doctorate in employment law - she took up her first position as a notary in Rotthalmünster in the district of Passau in 2012.
In 2016, she went on parental leave, followed by the move to Regensburg.
Since then, she has taken over representation services in various notarial offices.
When the position in Eggenfelden was advertised because of the approaching retirement of Lutz Feller, she saw the right time for the full professional re-entry, explains the 39-year-old.
The team of her predecessor, who in the future will take over the holiday representation in the notary's office, has completely taken over Dr. Stefanie Kramer - and has praise for the team of the law firm ready: "It's going great!"
The fact that she can rely on well-rehearsed processes here makes it easier for her to take care of the forthcoming relocation of the private hub.
In terms of kindergarten or crèche places, she has already contacted a local institution.
And if everything works out as desired, she hopes to be able to pursue her hobby here soon - and get to know the most beautiful jogging tracks of the Rottal.
BVB plans to the league start with a maximum of 15,000 spectators
Dortmund The largest German football stadium in Dortmund won't even be 20 per cent filled at the start of the season in the event of a fan return.
"Due to the distance regulation, which must be adhered to, we are currently planning with 12,000 to 15,000 spectators," Borussia Dortmund's managing director Carsten Cramer told the "Tagesspiegel".
Normally, the arena in the precinct city holds a little more than 81,000 spectators at Bundesliga games.
In last season's ghost games caused by the corona pandemic, BVB had lost around four million euros per game.
Cramer, responsible for marketing, digitization and internationalization at BVB, hopes that in addition to the normal spectator seats, some of the approximately 4000 seats in the hospitality area can also be opened again: "It is, of course, a blow to the office if these revenues break away completely".
In view of the ongoing corona crisis, everyone is currently "well advised to take a stable side-long for now".
"If many companies are not doing well and 6.8 million people are in short-term work in Germany alone, then the need for entertainment is limited," Cramer explained.
The head of marketing praised the BVB professionals for their partial salary waiver, which is intended to contribute to overcoming the crisis: "Of course, the players know that their salary waiver has a different effect than when we rely on short-time working 100 people who work in merchandising".
Tiny miracles: Early start to life with a few hundred grams
Hardly heavier than a packet of butter, just three tablespoons of blood in his entire body: that was Gabriel when he was born - he weighed exactly 273 grams.
The boy is one of around 400 premature babies who are cared for annually at the Graz Children's Clinic.
A quarter of them weigh less than 1,500 grams, a few - like Gabriel and his brothers Raphael and Michael - are extreme lightweights with not even a kilogram of birth weight.
They are so small and born so early that neither lungs, nor intestines, nor muscles are fully formed.
Your skin is sometimes so tender that they could hurt the electrodes of an ECG.
And they're so tiny that they sometimes wear doll's clothing and mini diapers made especially for them.
But not so small that they wouldn't be by voice - "and how," chuckles Berndt Urlesberger.
“Even if, of course, they don’t scream quite as loudly as big babies.”
When the whiteboard becomes a stage
Despite Corona theatre in the classroom: Shortly before the beginning of the 9th classes of the Sankt-Jakobus-Gymnasium in Abtsgmünd experienced the whiteboard as a stage.
Actress Julia Sylvester from the Theater of the City of Aalen presented to the pupils the production "Das Heimatkleid" - by video.
Subsequently, the pupils were able to exchange ideas via Skype with the actress and the director Winfried Tobias about the play and the performance.
The idea had been born for the performance format from the current Corona situation.
Teacher Teresa Nar was pleased with the enrichment of teaching.
Even if at the end everyone involved agreed that the direct encounter in the theatre and stage space would offer additional charms, you can also see the potential for contact in the schools in the theatre.
"If we continue to improve the technical framework, this could become an interesting addition to the game operation," emphasized director Winfried Tobias.
First round drawn: The TSV 1860 would meet Eintracht Frankfurt in the DFB-Pokal
Should the TSV 1860 qualify for the DFB-Pokal, the Lions will meet Eintracht Frankfurt in the first round.
This yielded to Sunday's draw.
Sixty's possible opponent in the first round of the DFB Cup is certain.
The Lions would meet Eintracht Frankfurt in a successful qualifier.
This resulted in the draw on Sunday evening at the ARD-Sportschau.
Due to the corona pandemic, the field of participants is not yet complete, placeholders were used in the draw.
Due to the voluntary renunciation of semi-final opponents FC Memmingen, the TSV 1860 is already in the final of the Bavarian Toto Cup (4 or 5 September).
There, the Lions meet either the Würzburg kickers or Viktoria Aschaffenburg.
Should Zweitliga runner-up Würzburg reach the final, Sixty would have already qualified for the first round in the DFB-Pokal.
Otherwise, the Giesingers would have to prevail in the final against regionalligist Aschaffenburg.
The first round of the DFB Cup will take place between 11 and 14 September and thus one week before the third league start.
'Gaven by the wind': Film legend Olivia de Havilland has died
Film legend and Oscar winner Olivia de Havilland has died.
She died on Sunday in Paris with 104 years of a natural death, confirmed her spokeswoman for the German press Agency.
De Havilland was known for her role as Melanie Hamilton in the southern epic "Gone from the Wind" (1939).
Born as a child of British parents in Tokyo, Olivia de Havilland moved to California as a toddler.
The Austrian theatreman Max Reinhardt discovered the 19-year-old in the role of Hermia of Shakespeare's "Summer Night's Dream."
Warner Brothers film studio contracted her for seven years and brought them out big alongside Eroll Flynn.
She made eight films with the heartbreaker.
In 1946, she won her first Oscar in the leading role of "To Each His Own."
The film ran in Germany under the title "Motherheart."
Three years later, the award came as best actress in William Wyler's "The Heiress."
She was also given a lot of recognition as a neurotic eccentric in "The Dark Mirror" and "The Snake Pit".
Daphne du Maurier proposed her in 1952 for the starring role in the adaptation of her novel "My Cousin Rachel."
The piece became a worldwide success.
With her long-term friend Bette Davis, she starred in "Lullaby for a Corpse."
From the marriage to the writer Marcus Goodrich (1946 to 1953), the son Benjamin emerged.
In Paris, de Havilland married journalist Pierre Paul Galante and had daughter Gisèle with him.
This marriage, too, broke down.
Rumors of an affair with former British Prime Minister Edward Heath, however, have never been confirmed.
"Road to Timmendorf": Beach volleyball: Thole/Wickler miss final on comeback
World Cup seconds Julius Thole and Clemens Wickler have missed the finals at the beach volleyball tournament "Road to Timmendorf".
In the semi-finals, they defeated their national team colleagues Nils Ehlers and Lars Flüggen with 1:2 (17:21, 21:17, 19:21).
Thole/Wickler last played at the World Tour Final in Rome in September last year.
In the final match on the Rhine, their conquerors Ehlers/Flüggen conceded a defeat against the surprise team Lukas Pfretschner and his interim partner Steven van de Velde with 0:2 (17:21, 17:21).
In addition to the show tournament, three starting places for the German championships were playeded at the beginning of September in Timmendorfer Strand: The tickets were secured by Armin Dollinger/Simon Kulzer, Manuel Harms/Richard Peemüller and Simon Pfretschner/Milan Sievers.
Kalbitz expulsion from AfD confirmed - FDP speaks of "fig leaf"
The exclusion of Andreas Kalbitz from the AfD is only a "fig leaf" from the FDP's point of view and does not change the character of the party.
“The AfD is the parliamentary arm of the far-right in Germany.
Andreas Kalbitz is only part of the tip of the iceberg of radicals in the AfD," FDP domestic politician Benjamin Strasser told the news agency dpa in Berlin.
The Federal Arbitration Court of the AfD had confirmed on Saturday in Stuttgart after several hours of hearing the exclusion of Kalbitz, which the Federal Executive had decided in May majority.
Kalbitz is chairman of the AfD faction in the Brandenburg state parliament.
"If the AfD were really about removing right-wing extremists consistently from their party, officials like Björn Höcke as well as his supporters would have been ruled out by now," Strasser said.
“However, there is no political will in the AfD because right-wing extremism now belongs to the party.”
CSU Secretary-General Markus Blume said: "That Andreas Kalbitz now remains excluded says nothing and does not change anything," he told Welt am Sonntag.
“Rather, it speaks volumes how many frenetic supporters a right-wing extremist in the middle of the AfD still has.”
Blume assumes that the AfD will land again below five percent in elections in the future.
“The days of the AfD in parliaments are numbered.”
Kalbitz was one of the spokespersons of the officially now dissolved "wing" around the Thuringian AfD state and faction chairman Björn Höcke; the constitutional protection classifies the trending as a "secure right-wing extremist aspiration".
In May, the AfD federal board had knocked Kalbitz out of the party.
As a reason, Kalbitz did not specify an earlier membership in the now banned far-right far-right "Heimattreuen Deutschen Jugend" (HDJ) (HDJ) during his party entrancement.
Kalbitz denies membership in the HDJ.
The chairman of the left-wing faction in the Brandenburg state parliament, Sebastian Walter, emphasized that the decision of the Federal Arbitration Court does not change the character of the AfD: "The wolf has only eaten chalk.
The AfD remains a far-right party, whether with or without Kalbitz.
With this decision, neither the federal party is weakened nor the right wing.
"The maneuver of the Meuthen squad is transparent.
One wants to escape further observation by the protection of the Constitution and try to get through under the radar," Walter said.
“The AfD can’t get rid of the spirits it called.
An expulsion doesn't change anything yet," the Brandenburg SPD General Secretary Erik Stohn commented on the decision in the Kalbitz case.
“Here, the AfD-Landesverband and the faction held to this day to their right-wing leader.”
Kalbitz's legal dispute over Kalbitz's membership in the AfD will continue.
He announced on Saturday that he would now take civil action against the decision of the Federal Arbitration Court.
Nordsee wind farms deliver a good 21 percent more electricity
The wind power plants on the German North Sea fed 11.51 terawatt hours of electricity into the grid in the first six months of year.
This is a good 21 percent more than in the same period of the previous year, the network operator Tennet in Bayreuth said.
Tennet is responsible for the transmission of the North Sea electricity to the mainland and the transmission through the German electricity grid.
The wind turbines on the Baltic Sea, which do not belong to Tennet's network area, fed in a further 2.22 terawatt-hours (TWh) of electricity.
In total, the wind power from the North Sea and the Baltic Sea thus reaches 13.73 TWh, compared to a good 11.64 TWh in the first half of 2019.
Wind harvesting at sea thus accounts for almost 19 percent of the total wind electricity in Germany; this is about four percentage points more than a year ago.
New wind farms ensure higher generation capacities
In addition to the wind conditions, the increased contribution of offshore wind powers results from higher generation capacities because new wind farms have been connected to the grid in the past twelve months.
If the 1270 wind power plants in the North Sea were all running at full power at the same time, they could generate 6.7 gigawatts of electricity mathematically.
The transmission capacity is sufficient for this; according to Tennet, it stands at 7.1 gigawatts.
By 2030, it is expected to rise to 17 gigawatts in the German North Sea.
"In Germany and the Netherlands, we are investing €20 billion in the expansion of offshore grid connections by then," said Tennet Managing Director Tim Meyerjürgens.
In reality, even at sea, wind farms can't exhaust their capacity, but reach about 40 percent.
The previous record day this year is January 2, when North Sea wind farms generated more than six gigawatts of electricity.
On average, North Sea wind turbines produce about 2.5 to 3.0 gigawatts of electricity, equivalent to three to four nuclear power plants.
This could supply almost seven million households with electricity.
Art "Skirmish" - an exhibition by the Leipzig artist Christian Bold in the gallery of the Gmünder Kunstverein.
This was almost a small anniversary celebration for the 130.
Birthday of the Gmünder Kunstverein: The exhibition opening "Skirmish" with works by the Leipzig artist Christian Bold blasted the usual framework after the Corona break.
Many more art lovers than usual followed the dialogue between the artist and the chairman of the Kunstverein, Professor Dr. Klaus Ripper.
The band "Defenders of the Universe" provided the musical equivalent on southern Kornhausplatz.
North of the gallery, the association "Sandtogether" was involved.
Freital: Windbergfest cancelled due to Corona
Windbergfest will not take place this year.
The city of Freital has cancelled the three-day festival planned in September.
In other years, over 30,000 people had attended this city festival.
"We really didn't make this decision easy for ourselves and - aware of how difficult it is economically for those involved - hoped until the last minute," explained Lord Mayor Uwe Rumberg (non-party).
“But in the end, as the organizers, we are responsible for the health of many thousands of people.”
If tens of tens of thousands had flowed onto the festival grounds and into the marquee, minimum distances, hygiene regulations and a contact follow-up in accordance with the current corona protection regulation could not have been complied with.
Or the restrictions would have been so severe that not much would have been left of a folk festival.
In addition, the organizational and monitoring effort would increase disproportionately by a multiple.
Rumberg comforted on next year.
Then the city of Freital celebrates its 100th anniversary, among other things, with the Winterbergfest from 7 to 9 May 2021 as well as the Day of the Saxons from 10 to 12 September 2021.
Dressage Olympic champion Werth not to beat in Achleiten
The six-time Olympic champion Isabell Werth was the outstanding rider at the dressage tournament in the Austrian Achleiten.
The 51-year-old from Rheinberg scored four wins at four starts at the weekend.
After winning a Grand Prix with Weihegold and Quantaz, she could not be beaten on Sunday in the Grand Prix Special and the freestyle.
On the 15-year-old mare Weihegold, Werth prevailed clearly against the Austrian Victoria Max-Theurer on Abegglen on Sunday in the special.
Also in the freestyle, the German on Saturday on Quantaz was clearly ahead of her 34-year-old student Victoria Max-Theurer.
Werth's winning horse Quantaz belongs to Max-Theurer.
The world's most successful dressage rider is to establish the ten-year-old stallion in the Grand Prix.
For Quantaz, it was the first freeze ever.
Werth had already won all the important four competitions at the first tournament in Achleiten three weeks ago.
The last of the three international tournaments is scheduled there for August 14-16.
In the future, more air traffic controllers in Worms
The Worms University of Applied Sciences continues to the air: "DFS Deutsche Flugsicherung" donates a professorship for "Air Traffic Management".
A corresponding contract between the university and the DFS has now been signed in Worms.
The contract was initially finalised for three years and starts in 2021.
With the professorship there is the possibility to enable the start of studies not only in the summer, but also in the winter semester.
Thus, twice as many students can be trained as before.
At the same time, the capacities in both the university and the DFS air traffic control academy are used evenly.
The DFS will advise the university on the establishment and appointment of the professorship.
Represented at the university since 2019
Since the summer semester 2019, the first students have been part of their training as an air traffic controller at Worm’s University of Applied Sciences in Air Traffic Management.
This year, nine students have already started.
So far, the experience of the DFS is very positive, the performance of the students is excellent.
The combination of studies and pilot training contributes significantly to increasing the attractiveness of the pilot profession.
“In these times, the promotion is not easy for us economically.
However, we firmly expect air traffic to recover and grow again in the long term.
In order to be able to fulfil our legal mandate in the future, we need highly qualified personnel," says Professor Klaus-Dieter Scheurle, CEO of DFS when signing the contract.
"For DFS, the endowed professorship is a future-providing investment that will provide air traffic controllers with a broad, cross-disciplinary knowledge base in the long term.
We are therefore continuing good cooperation with the university and at the same time underiving our attractiveness as an employer
This is also emphasized by Professor Dr. Jens Hermsdorf, President of Worm’s University of Applied Sciences: “The endowed professorship forms an important bridge between science and industry.
With it, we would like to build on the already existing cooperation with DFS Deutsche Flugsicherung as a practical partner for the dual bachelor's degree program Air Traffic Management."
At the same time, the trust of German air traffic control is Worm’s university’s motivation and obligation to further expand its teaching and research competence in the future fields of air transport.
With the first endowered professorship for a dual course of study, the university can set very special highlights and innovative priorities for its students for the air traffic controllers of tomorrow.
Dr. Denis Alt, State Secretary in the Ministry of Science, Continuing Education and Culture, stresses: "Worm's University of Applied Sciences has a unique selling point in Rhineland-Palatinate with its focus in the field of air transport.
Today's signing of the cooperation agreement strengthens the profile of the university".
Rescue helicopter lands on the A 45
For about 30 minutes, the A 45 motorway was fully closed on Saturday noon in the direction of Aschaffenburg, as the rescue helicopter was in use after a traffic accident.
A 76-year-old drove with a Ford at around 12.40 p.m. in the Alzenau-Mitte exit in the curve and landed at the guardrail.
Due to the impact, the front airbags triggered, the driver suffered minor injuries to his face and had to be taken to a hospital for treatment.
The injuries of the 72-year-old passenger were taken care of by the emergency doctor and the patient was subsequently flowned with the rescue helicopter to a hospital.
Currently, however, the police assume that there is no danger to life.
The damaged vehicle, which caused damage of about 2000 euros, had to be towed away.
Heil wants to examine other industries according to meat industry
The corona crisis once again brought to light the conditions in parts of the meat industry.
Legislations are imminent.
But are they right - and will it stay with it?
Federal Labour Minister Hubertus Heil (SPD) wants to check other industries according to the now planned strict regulations against grievances in the meat industry.
"We will look at industry for industry and then take appropriate measures for the respective industry if necessary," Heil told the German Press Agency in Berlin.
Germany's employers are already warning against restricting work contracts in general.
After the mass corona outbreak at the meat giant Tönnies in North Rhine-Westphalia, the Federal Cabinet wants to adopt a law from Heil this Wednesday.
Large-scale slaughterhouses should no longer be allowed to use foreign personnel during slaughter, dismantling and meat processing.
For this purpose, the use of contract workers and temporary workers is to be prohibited there.
Employer president Ingo Kramer told the dpa: "Fingers away from a general restriction of work contracts and temporary employment if you do not want to choke off the economy completely".
Work contracts are, for example, the basis for large areas of crafts, the construction industry and plant construction, for their added value at the customer.
"The misconductions of individuals cannot destroy such an important instrument."
Heil assured: "My goal is not to ban work contracts everywhere in the German economy".
Work contracts make sense.
"If an industrial company commissions a craftsman to install a safety system, this is a completely normal work contract."
The German meat industry considers a ban on work contracts and temporary agency work solely unconstitutional in its industry.
It is inexplicable why different employment law should apply in the case of portioning and packaging of cheese in the future than with sausage, according to a statement on the legislative project, as the "Neue Osnabrücker Zeitung" (Saturday) reports.
In the case of a ban, the consumer price would increase by 10 to 20 percent per kilo and product.
Many Eastern European employees from factory contractors worked 16 hours a day in the industry according to the results of an audit by the NRW-Arbeitsschutzverwaltung.
Often they didn't have a break.
Wages were withheld for protective equipment or rent.
It was also noted that the situation was often miserable in the accommodation of employees.
Heil said a relatively sharp law had already come into effect for the meat industry in 2017.
However, "trictical constructions" were bypassed.
“Now it has to be fundamentally cleaned up.”
The politician announced: "In the Occupational Health and Safety Act, which we want to decide in the cabinet at the end of July, there are also measures that not only affect the meat industry - for example, higher test rates for occupational safety or clear criteria for community accommodation."
The dpa’s draft includes amendments to the law on safeguarding workers’ rights in the meat industry as well as the Occupational Safety and Health Act.
Brandenburg's Consumer Protection Minister Ursula Nonnemacher (Greens) considers more intensive checks necessary.
"I would like us to be able to do even more controls in many supervisory areas: food controls, animal welfare, everything possible, for example also animal transports," Nonnemacher said on the RBB broadcast "Brandenburg aktuell" on Sunday.
However, more personnel positions are also needed for more checks.
Three killed in crash of ultra-light aircraft in Germany
Three people have died in the crash of an ultra-light aircraft on an apartment building in Wesel in the German state of North Rhine-Westphalia, according to the police.
One child was slightly injured.
Two other people had been rescued injured in the accident, said a spokesman for the district control center Wesel.
A parachute had been found at the scene of the accident, according to police.
The "Rheinische Post" reported, citing eyewitnesses, that before the crash, the plane collided with a hot air balloon.
The house is a single or two-family house.
Further details were not initially disclosed.
Eviction of Frankfurter Opernplatz proceeds peacefully
One week after the riots in Frankfurt, the eviction of the Opernplatz during the night on Sunday has also proceeded peacefully.
Most recently, a few dozen people were still in the square, but left it at 1 a.m. prior to the curfew began.
The police had been on duty with numerous officers since the early evening, in the entire urban area could be seen a larger police line-up.
For the first time this weekend, from midnight, there was a ban on entry for the Opera Square, on which there had been riots a week ago.
Officers had been attacked with bottles from the crowd of revelers.
At least five officers were injured after police entries, several police vehicles damaged.
The night on Saturday had also remained calm.
Car rolls over: Young woman seriously injured in accident on Bundesstraße 83 near Hofgeismar
In an accident between Schöneberg and Hümme, a 21-year-old suffered serious injuries at about 13:25 on Sunday.
She was taken to the Klinikum Kassel.
Using thermal imaging cameras, firefighters searched for possible passengers.
According to police, the woman was travelling on Bundesstraße 83 in the direction of her place of residence Hümme when her car came off the carriageway for a previously unexplained cause and overturned several times.
In order to rescue the accident, the fire brigade had to clear the roof of the car.
Since it was unclear whether anyone was still in the vehicle, the adjacent corn field was searched from the revolving ladder with a thermal imaging camera.
The drone squadron from Warburg, which also works with thermal imaging cameras, had also been ordered for the person search.
However, the emergency services did not find anyone between the high maize plants.
The seriously injured person was taken by ambulance to the Kassel hospital for further care.
Nearly 50 emergency personnel from the Hofgeismar, Hümme and Schöneberg firefight were on site.
The federal highway was closed for two hours due to the accident.
Juventus Turin champion in Italy
After a redemptive goal from Cristiano Ronaldo, record champion Juventus Turin has secured the Italian championship for the ninth time in a row.
The team around the five-time world footballer made the 36th scudetto in the club's history perfect with a 2:0 (1:0) on Sunday night in the home game against Sampdoria Genoa.
Ronaldo scored 1:0 before half-time.
With 83 points, Turin, which competed without the last long-injured ex-national player Sami Khedira, is no longer ousted from number one two matchdays before the end of Serie A season.
The second-placed Inter Milan is seven points behind.
Juventus were harmless despite visual superiority in the first half, then Ronaldo scored after a free-kick variant with the last action.
The 35-year-old exploited an input from Miralem Pjanic from the penalty area boundary directly with a flact shot (45.+7).
Against the now more offensive guests Ronaldo was also involved in the 2:0, after his shot Federico Bernardeschi (67th) dusted off.
This ensured the lacklustre but decisive success.
The foul penalty in the 89th minute, which was fired to the bar, is likely to only Ronaldo himself with a view to the goal-scorining.
“It’s complicated to stay mentally and physically at ball height for 90 minutes.
The games are strange, and the momentum changes very easily," coach Maurizio Sarri explained even after 1:2 at Udinese Calcio on Thursday.
Above all, the mental exhaustion of his players is great, the Juve coach said.
Because of the defeat, the Turinians had not yet been able to make the championship perfect.
Juve recently owed the reassuring lead at the top of the standings due to the weakness of the competitors.
Because in the eleven games before Sunday's game, the Sarri team had managed only five wins.
Whether Ronaldo will also become top scorer is questionable.
While the Portuguese superstar has scored 31 goals so far, the former Dortmund player Ciro Immobile already has 34 goals on his account after his three-pack at 5:1 (1:1) of fourth-placed Lazio Rome at Hella's Verona - as much as Robert Lewandowski contributed to Bayern's title win.
The record is held by the current Juve professional Gonzalo Higuaín.
The Argentine scored 36 goals for SSC Naples in the 2015/2016 season.
In August, the "Old Lady" of Italian football awaits the next title opportunity.
Juventus is in the quarter-finals of the Champions League, but will have to make up for a 0:1 first-leg break against Olympique Lyon on 7 August to reach the final tournament in Lisbon.
In the next knockout round, Real Madrid or Manchester City would be waiting as opponents.
Baden-Württemberg: Over 250 illegal car races recorded
Stuttgart - Despite all threatened penalties and court rulings, motorists continue to give gas during countless illegal races on Baden-Württemberg's roads: Last year and the first four months of this year, the police in the southwest have already registered more than 250 illegal car races.
Comparative values for the years before do not exist, but the racers are doing increasingly work for the judicial authorities: In the public prosecutor’s offices between Konstanz and Mannheim, a total of 155 investigations were conducted for illegal races in 2018, a year later there were already 252 and in the current year until the beginning of June 151.
The races were less about organized events, according to the response of the Interior Ministry to a request from the Landtag SPD.
In most cases, the drivers spontaneously competed against each other or they had taken flight from the police - this is also indicated as an illegal race.
German men between the ages of 18 and 60 were sitting at the wheel.
113 fines - 15 times imprisoned
Only in comparatively a few cases, the racers are caught and also asked to pay: According to the Interior Ministry, a total of 113 fines have been impossed since 2018 and 15 imprisonments have been pronounced.
However, there are no indications that the number of illegal car races is actually increasing.
Rather, other motorists reacted more sensitively than before because of the several tragic accidents and processes of previous years and indicated more frequently, said a ministry spokesman.
Especially popular with razors is the highway 8. Again and again, motorists brake the traffic there in order to gain space and use the free track for a race.
Last August, a dozen drivers near Kirchheim under Teck were caught, three months earlier there were even 18 drivers on the A8 near Merklingen.
A81 is the most popular "racing track"
On the A81, the police even register more races than on any other federal highway.
With a speed limit, an attempt is made to slow down the racers on the particularly affected section between Engen and Geisingen.
The mostly high-priced and puffed-up sports and luxury bodies often have Swiss license plates, because the drivers dodge to Germany because of the high penalties for too fast driving and car racing in the neighboring country.
For the SPD, the speed limit must not be reduced to a few routes: "With a general speed limit of 130 kilometers per hour, we would manage this problem on motorways," says Martin Rivoir, the transport expert of the SPD parliamentary group.
“The inhibition threshold for racers would be significantly higher if you were not allowed to be legally here and there with 200 kilometers per hour and more on the road.”
Rivoir thinks less of enlightenment or image campaigns: "Who defies all the rules in this way and puts not only one's own life, but also the lives of other road users at risk, can hardly be reached with reason".
ADAC calls for greater crackdown by police
On the other hand, this is not enough for ADAC: "We assume that racers will not be deterred by the speed limits," says Holger Bach, head of the transport and environment department at ADAC Württemberg.
He is calling for additional and also tougher checks on the affected routes.
It is not uncommon for the high-speed show of force to become more painful and consequential seriousness: Since January 2019 and until April 2020, three people have died in the races nationwide, two of them uninvolved in a nocturnal crash in Stuttgart’s city centre and one in Gerlingen.
Two young men are said to have delivered a car race there in November.
One of the vehicles came off the road, bounced against a garden wall and remained lying on its side, the 18-year-old passenger died.
21 other people have been injured in the races of the past two years.
According to Interior Minister Thomas Strobl, however, stricter penalties are not necessary: "Legislative action to sanction illegal motor vehicle races harder is not seen," according to the paper of the Interior Ministry.
July 23 blue light: Thirsty burglar steals drinks from takeaway
Rohrdorf: A man broke into a takeaway in Rohrdorf (Calw district) on Thursday and stole drinks and cash.
A witness heard noises in the valley road at around 3.40 a.m. and ran towards the takeaway.
There he noticed a man using drinks at the fridge and subsequently left the takeaway through a previously smashed-in entrance door.
The perpetrator fled in the direction of the meadows behind the takeaway.
The burglar also used a cash register, according to police, stole cash in the low three-figure range.
The suspect is described as follows: He is about 1.75 to 1.80 meters tall and of strong stature.
In fact, he wore a black hooded sweater, black trousers and light-coloured shoes.
The police receive further witnesses.
Ackermann sprints to victory on final stage at Sibiu Tour
Cyclist Pascal Ackermann won his second day on the final stage of the 10th Sibiu Tour.
The 26-year-old Palatinate from Team Bora-hansgrohe prevailed on the 109-kilometre section with the start and finish in Hermannstadt in the mass sprint, having already won a stage on Saturday.
It was the season victories three and four for Ackermann.
In the overall standings, his Austrian teammate Gregor Mühlberger prevailed.
The 26-year-old had confidently decided the mountain time trial for himself in the morning and thus took over the top in the classification.
Only 104 drivers had competed for the final stage in the evening.
No less than 18 starters had fallen victim to the time limit in the mountain time trial.
"I'm really happy to start the season restart with a win," Ackermann had declared after his success on Saturday.
"The sprint was really hectic," the 26-year-old said.
300 meters before the finish, he simply put on his sprint, "and it also worked out perfectly".
According to the rules of the world association UCI, a corona test was carried out for all race participants and their companions six days before the start of the race.
During the complete tour, drivers and supervisors in isolated hygiene bladders were separated from the organizing team as well as from the media.
Spectators at the start and finish were not allowed.
control center gave instruction for lay resuscitation
Duesdesduls - Tragic accident on Sunday afternoon in Düsseldorf-Garath.
In an apartment building, a child (4) suddenly lay lifeless in the water while bathing.
According to the fire department, the emergency call was received at just before 1 p.m.
"A control center dispatcher immediately gave instructions on lay resuscitation," a firefighting spokesman told BILD.
The incoming rescue workers took over the resuscitation measures a little later.
The child floating in danger of death was transported to the university hospital under resuscitation.
Why it crashed is unclear.
Police have routinely commenced investigations into the accident.
"We assume a tragic accident in the apartment".
Nuremberg: Unapproved tuning meeting - police intervene, 700 vehicles and around 1100 people
Hundreds of cars and temporarily over 1000 people took part in a "tuning meeting" in the parking lot of a furniture store on Saturday night.
The police implemented their announcement to control such events more closely.
A major tuning meeting took place on Saturday evening (July 25, 2020) in Nuremberg's southern city.
Several hundred vehicles were involved.
According to Mittelfranken police, an increased number of vehicles in the area of a large parking lot in Ingolstädter Straße had been detected by the Nuremberg traffic police around 22.30.
As a result, the police carried out a variety of vehicle checkpoints there.
Only recently, the police had announced that they wanted to take stronger action against so-called autoposers.
Illegal tuning meeting in Nuremberg: Only a small part from the "scene"
The officers counted a good 700 vehicles and about 1100 people, reports the police.
However, only a small proportion of those present can be attributable to the "autoposer" and tuning scene.
Apparently, the majority of the relevant scene complied with the appeal of the police.
On the basis of the local vehicles and persons, it can be assumed that these were predominantly onlookers.
In addition, the vast majority of those present adhered to the applicable infection protection regulations, in particular complying with the minimum distance and wearing a mouth-nose protection.
The on-site control measures were completed around 1:00 a.m.
In total, the officials noticed such structural changes in 10 vehicles that the operating license had expired.
Four of these vehicles had to be secured.
No car racing
There were no illegal car races.
"The Central Franconia Police Headquarters will continue to keep an eye on what's happening and will continue to carry out increased control measures, including in connection with the advent of onlookers," it said.
The agency News5 spoke to Jürgen Schrödinger from the towing company involved: "They all have beautiful rims, we must not scratch them," says Schrödinger.
Special bands would be used for this.
Also because the cars are wider, the company must be careful not to cause any damage.
"After all, the young people are supposed to get their cars back unscathed."
Employment agency figures: 900 suspected cases of short-time abuse
The Federal Employment Agency (BA), according to its own statement, investigates around 900 suspected cases of abuse in short-term workers' pay.
"This is extremely little with more than 880,000 companies that have indicated short-time working," Daniel Terzenbach's board member responsible for the operational business told the Frankfurter Allgemeine Zeitung newspaper.
At the beginning of the corona crisis, many companies suddenly applied for short-time work, the BA had to react quickly.
Normally the exam lasts up to three weeks.
“There was not so much time available to us during this hot phase.
All the more closely, we will look at the final settlements.
I'm sure there's going to be a lot more.
We’ren’t naïve there and have a long breath.”
Evidence of abuse would often come from the affected employees or unions.
In addition, the BA uses analysis software to detect patterns of abuse.
According to Terzenbach, the BA is preparing for a possible wave of insolvency in the autumn.
“Will there be significantly more insolvencies than in the previous year?
The assessments differ, but we are preparing ourselves in the event of the case and are already training our employees in the employment agencies for the processing of applications for insolvency money.”
The numbers are still unobtrusive, Terzenbach said.
World Heritage in Potsdam: Party in the New Garden dissolved
Potsdam - On Friday evening, about 40 teenagers celebrated a party in the New Garden.
The collection had been disbanded at 20.48 by the district drivers of the Prussian Castles and Gardens (SPSG Foundation), the foundation said on PNN request.
The young people left the park.
On Saturday night, some youngsters would have played rugby in the New Garden.
The meadow had been cleared by precinct drivers, SPSG spokesman Frank Kallensee said on Sunday.
Again and again there are illegal celebrations in the parks
In the parks of the foundation there are always accumulations in which the distance and hygiene rules Covid-195 circumvention regulation are not complied with.
At the end of June there was a celebration with about 300 people in the Park Babelsberg, a week later about 150 people celebrated in the New Garden.
Even without a corona ordinance, the parks will be closed at nightfall.
The storage on the meadows is prohibited according to parking regulations, there are few exceptions.
The foundation has complained for a long time that visitors would leave more and more rubbish and destroy protected biotopes.
In Berlin, meanwhile, illegal parking parties have reached a different dimension.
In the Hasenheide in the district of Neukölln, 5000 people celebrated an illegal techno party during the night to Sunday.
The event was disbanded by the police.
Austria: mass tests after corona outbreak in tourist resort
Authorities have reacted with hundreds of tests to a corona outbreak in the popular Upper Austrian tourist resort of St. Wolfgang.
The number of confirmed cases rose to 48, as the state authorities in Linz announced.
More than 100 results were pending in the evening.
Accordingly, many of the infected are young interns in tourism companies, but at least one holiday guest has also tested positive.
Residents, guests and tourism staff were asked to stay in their shelters on Saturday night.
The curfew was set at 23 o'clock.
"There are cancellations and cancellations there and there" of bookings, said the head of the tourism association Wolfgangsee, Hans Wieser, the dpa.
Also, occasional guests left prematurely.
St. Wolfgang is the most important tourist place in Upper Austria, with more than 400,000 overnight stays last year.
In a normal season, around one-third of guests are German.
On Friday, the first infections had become publicly known.
The head of the district authority, Alois Lanz, on Saturday defended the behavior of the interns, who had celebrated in several establishments and may have become infected there.
"We all enjoyed doing this in our youth," he told radio station Ö1.
However, two bars were closed on Friday to prevent further infections.
The tourism board launched an information campaign to sensitize young employees to the dangers of Covid-19.
From Saturday, hundreds of employees, holidaymakers and locals were tested.
In addition, all guests who were in St. Wolfgang from July 15 were contacted.
The eruption evokes memories of the Austrian ski and party venue Ischgl in Tyrol, which in the initial phase of the pandemic was a center of corona spread throughout Europe.
In total, more than 1500 people are currently infected with the virus in Austria.
Focus on new infections are Vienna and Upper Austria.
Premier League: Man United and Chelsea qualified for Champions League
London (dpa) - The English football record champion Manchester United returns to the Champions League after a year absence.
The Red Devils secured third place in the table on the last matchday of the Premier League through a 2:0 (0:0) -success against direct competitor Leicester City.
The Foxes now have to settle for the Europa League because Chelsea still moved past and also qualified for the premier class in fourth place.
In the fight against relegation, Aston was allowed to cheer.
Manchester United went into the lead through a penalty kick from Bruno Fernandes (71st minute).
After a red card for Leicester City in the aftermath, Jesse Lingard (90.+8) scored.
Chelsea, in the Champions League quarter-finals opponents of Bayern, won thanks to Mason Mount (45.+1) and Olivier Giroud (45.+4) in parallel with 2:0 (2:0) against the Wolverhampton Wanderers.
"That means very much to me," said coach Frank Lampard, who in his first season as Chelsea coach is also in the FA Cup's final against Arsenal.
Tottenham Hotspur also benefited from Chelsea's victory against Wolverhampton, which still moved past the Wolves with a meagre 1:1 (1:0) at Crystal Palace and starts sixth in the Europa League.
Aston Villa decided the long-distance duel with Watford and the AFC Bournemouth for himself, but had to tremble at West Ham United for a long time.
Jack Grealish's late lead (84th) promptly equalized Andrej Yarmolenko (85th).
The 1:1 (0:0) was enough, however, because Watford lost with 2:3 (1:3) at Arsenal.
The club, whiching three coaches in the season, relegates after five first-league years.
Just like Bournemouth, who couldn't save himself through a 3:1 (2:1) at Everton.
In the end, a point was missing.
FC Liverpool, which has been the English champion since the end of June, secured a club record with a 3:1 (1:1) victory at Newcastle United.
99 points is the highest the Reds have ever scored in a football season.
Coach Jürgen Klopp narrowly missed the points record in English football with his team.
The record holds Manchester City with 100 points in the 2017/18 season.
Virgil van Dijk (38.), Divock Origi (59.) and Sadio Mané (89.) turned the game on Sunday for Liverpool.
"For this wonderful club, it's been an extraordinary season," goalscorer van Dijk said after the game, "that should spur us to defend the title.
It's going to be hard."
The Premier League bosses are also likely to be satisfied in the end.
Due to the coronavirus crisis, football operations in England were suspended from March 13 to June 17.
In the meantime, there was even discussion about an early demolition or a hosting of the remaining games on neutral ground.
Finally, however, the season could be played without any special events - but as in Germany without spectators in the stadiums.
Arrests and Violence at Demonstrations Against Netanyahu
The protests in Israel against Netanyahu's head of government are becominging increasingly fierce.
Violent clashes occurred during the night.
Israeli police have cracked down violently cracked down on a demonstration against right-wing Conservative Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu during the night of Sunday in Jerusalem.
Twelve people have been arrested for participating in riots, police spokesman Micky Rosenfeld said.
Among other things, the police used water cannons to dissolve the rally.
In total, around 6000 people had gathered near Netanyahu's headquarters, according to media reports on Saturday night.
They protested against unemployment, corruption and Netanyahu's handling of the Corona crisis.
The protesters slapped drums, blew into horns and called on Netanyahu to resign.
Participants of a counter-demonstration that supported Netanyahu were also arrested, the news site "ynet" reported.
In many other places in Israel, people also demonstrated, including in front of the head of government's private mansion in the coastal city of Caesarea and in a park in Tel Aviv.
A man has been arrested near Tel Aviv, according to police information, because it is under suspicion of spraying pepper spray on protesters on the side of the road.
Another man has been arrested following a knife attack on a protester in the south of the country.
The protester had been slightly injured in the neck.
There have been several demonstrations against Netanyahu in Jerusalem and Tel Aviv.
The rallies accompany the corruption process against the head of government.
Most recently, Netanyahu was also criticized for his handling of the corona crisis.
Among other things, he is accused of hasty easing and a lack of preparation for a second corona wave.
The Corona crisis has severely affected the country's economy.
The unemployment rate last stood at more than 20 percent.
The number of new infections with the coronavirus in Israel has recently risen to ever new records.
Accident near Ahlen (NRW) - Van crashes on car - nine injured - Ruhr area - Bild.de
Ahlen - A rear-end collision on Saturday afternoon on the B58 near Ahlen claimed nine lightly injured.
A female motorist was traveling with her two children (3, 5) towards Beckum.
The police: "Presumably she had suddenly braked because she wanted to turn".
The driver of a Mercedes van who was driving behind her must have seen this too late.
He crashed into the back of the car.
Three occupants in the car and six in the van were slightly injured in the accident.
Several ambulances took them to a surrounding hospital.
North Korea locks down border town after Corona suspected case
North Korea has so far seen a "luminous success" in its own measures to prevent the coronavirus.
Now, for the first time, the country is reporting a suspected case.
Due to a first official suspected corona case in the country, North Korea has completely sealed off a big city for fear of a disaster.
Despite strict epidemiological defensive measures in the past six months, "the vicious virus could have been introduced into the country," state media reported.
The state of emergency had been imposed on the region around the border town of Kaesong.
The Communist leadership proclaimed the highest level of alert.
If the suspected case is confirmed, it would be the first official case of infection with Sars-CoV-2 in the sealed-off country.
North Korea had so far not registered a single infection and described its defensive measures as a "glowing success."
The information was doubted abroad.
The symptoms of the disease were reportedly detected in an "outlier" who illegally crossed the military demarcation line between South and North Korea on July 19.
The person defected to South Korea three years ago and has now returned across the border.
There was initially no confirmation for the border crossing within the four-kilometer-wide military buffer zone, which was still mined for the border.
South Korea's military, however, acknowledged the possibility of an illegal border crossing into North Korea as high, according to reports by the national news agency Yonhap.
Investigations were carried out on some people.
According to North Korea on Saturday called an emergency meeting of the Politburo of the Workers' Party, chaired by Kim Jong Un.
"The dangerous situation in Kaesong could trigger a deadly and destructive disaster."
"Kim had already ordered on Friday as a preventive measure to "completely block Kaesong and isolate every district and the region from others," it said.
The affected person, as well as all the people in Kaesong who had had contact with her, had been quarantined.
The central military commission of the party should therefore investigate the "outlier case" as well as the behavior of the responsible military unit and, if necessary, impose harsh punishment.
Kaesong is estimated to have about 300,000 people.
Tension ahead of council meeting: City-East and noise protection are still under discussion ahead
"In addition to environmental issues, the committee members will also vote, among other things, on the development plan for the large-scale downtown development-Ost project," reads incidentally in the middle section of the municipal press release for the next municipal council meeting on Tuesday, July 28, at 4.30 p.m. in the Great Hall of the CongressCentrum Pforzheim.
Above all, however, the appreciation of the eastern city is controversial until the end.
The majorities for this are scarce, most recently in individual committees even rejecting depending on attendance.
An appeal from the economy (construction, real estate, commerce and IT), the project developed out of the citizenry and then by the administration and the local council not to fail after almost ten years of preparation, criticized project opponent Reinhard Klein as an "extra-parliamentary opposition", "unsuspecting", "speculation", "vilification" and "conscious false statements".
"Everyone thinks he knows better," says Klein, who has been the only representative of Pforzheim's Citizens' List to the local council for a year.
In fact, after reading the treaties in this position: "I know what I'm talking about".
The exit would make the city cheaper, the technical town hall, contrary to statements of the city administration after grants, the existence of which Klein is convinced to refurbish for 8.5 million euros.
The city will develop very differently than is predicted in the town hall and by the proponents from the economy.
To shape the city through its own power and not an investor: This was his plan B. One day after Klein, the FDP/FW/UB/LED group, which also rejects the project, presented an alternative concept.
It essentially also envisages the renovation of the Technical Town Hall, the opening of the Schloßberg, the rental of the ex-thale for administrative areas and a more small-scale downtown redevelopment ideally with local sponsors.
Environmental issues play a central role
In addition, environmental issues in particular will play a central role.
Among other things, the local council will vote on the noise action plan.
This includes various measures to protect the Pforzheim population from health noise pollution caused by road traffic, including many speed reductions.
In a press release, the local Verkehrsclub Deutschland welcomes the widespread introduction of tempo 30. However, the political leaders in the city are not innocent of the fact that this must happen, because by neglecting the scheduled bus traffic with thinned cycles and through little commitment to cycling, they have only contributed to the massive increase in car traffic and thus of noise, according to the VCD.
The municipal council also votes on the participation of the city of the energy and climate protection agency Pforzheim/Enzkreis.
In addition, on the agenda is the accession to the climate protection pact of the State of Baden-Württemberg, the re-establishment of parental contributions in the municipal day-care facilities and the application for inclusion in the smart city funding of the federal government.
Citizens are welcome, but must heed corona protection measures.
With a lot of delay, Tim Zimmermann starts the new season
With almost half a year delay, Tim Zimmermann starts on July 31 in his premiere season at the ADAC GT Masters.
Before the league of super sports cars can start regular operation, the first test drives were scheduled.
For Zimmermann, it was all about getting to get to know his Lamborghini Huracan GT3.
The Corona crisis has for a long time prevented Tim Zimmermann from embarking on his first service ride in the new Lamborghini Huracan of Grasser Racing.
Under special hygiene conditions, this was now possible.
The 23-year-old Langenargener made his first laps at the Lausitzring and was able to get used to his premiere in the GT3 car.
"There are already a few things that are different than with Porsche from pre-season," says Zimmermann.
"With the ABS, for example, you have to go into the curves differently.
But it was really a lot of fun.”
Zimmermann also had fun with Steijn Schothorst, with whom he will share his orange sports car in the GT Masters.
Despite his only 25 years, the Dutchman is already experienced in GT racing and has been able to help his new team-mate in many things.
"I'm not a beginner in motorsport, but every new series also brings new challenges," says Zimmermann.
“Steijn and I understand each other brilliantly and we have also made enormous progress from session to session.”
Exactly there - at the Lausitzring - the GT Masters will also host its first race.
From 31 July, the 33 supercars will race through Lusatia.
Zimmermann, who is one of 15 newcomers to the series, knows the track, however.
In 2015 he was last a guest at Motodrom.
"It worked quickly and I didn't have much to get used to the track," says Zimmermann, who starts for Friedrichshafen's Graf Zeppelin racing community.
“The Lausitzring has many fast passages, which you have to meet optimally in order to be fast”.
How fast Zimmermann really is then, that turns out in two weeks.
"Of course, the teams play poker a bit in the tests," he says.
"In the end, however, we are very satisfied and are looking forward to the first weekend at the Lausitzring."
The race in Brandenburg will be the prelude to a densely crowded calendar.
There will be seven races until the beginning of November.
At the moment point is that no spectators will be allowed - but there is a celebrity guest.
In mid-August at the Nürburgring, ex-Formula 1 driver Niko Hülkenberg will reach into the GT steering wheel.
British transport minister caught cold by quarantine decision
The British government's decision to quarantine Spain holidaymakers for two weeks after their return has caught the country's transport minister cold, according to press reports.
Grant Shapps is currently on holiday in Spain, while his colleagues at home on Saturday decided the measure.
Due to the rising corona numbers again in Spain, the cabinet in London removed the country from the list of those countries from which entry without quarantine conditions is possible.
The new scheme will apply as of today, Sunday, so Shapps will also have to quarantine after his return.
In Austria, the government has also imposed quarantine measures for returnees.
But the scheme applies to those who are just on holiday on the cut-off date, only from Saturday, August 1, and has not been introduced overnight.
By the way, Spain has so far been considered one of the "safe countries" for which no quarantine in Austria is required.
"Numerous ministers are likely to have known in advance that there is a possibility that a quarantine will be imposed for holidaymakers Spain," commented Labour MP Diane Abbot.
“But apparently nobody bothered to tell Grant Shapps.”
Barely a month after the end of the corona emergency in Spain and the expiry of the strict curfew, the number of infections in the country is rising again.
Virologists mainly blame the nightlife for the renewed spread.
Spain is one of the UK's favourite holiday destinations.
The British government is currently discounting unnecessary travel to mainlinks, the Canary Islands and Balearic Islands, which are particularly popular among British holidaymakers.
More than 272,000 infections with the novel coronavirus and more than 28,000 deaths have been registered in Spain so far.
Russia: Navy Day: Putin announces hypersonic weapons for warships
Russian President Vladimir Putin has announced for the day of the navy in his country an equipment of the warships with state-of-the-art hypersonic weapons.
With these weapons unique in the world, the fighting possibilities of the Russian naval forces would be expanded, Putin said on Sunday in St. Petersburg at the central naval parade.
According to Interfax, the Ministry of Defense also informed the agency about successful tests of the hypersonic missile Zircon built for the Navy.
The hypersonic weapons have a multiple of the sound speed of around 343 meters per second.
They are considered weapons of the future.
Underwater tropedeo will continue to be built
It will continue to be built on the atomically equipped underwater torpedo Poseidon, it said.
The planned sea drone is planned to destroy coastal destinations.
The president, who himself also rode a military boat, said that this year alone 40 new ships would be put into service at the individual fleet associations of the largest country on earth.
Russia has such a large coastal area like no other country in the world.
For 300 years, the navy has reliably protected Russia, Putin said at the day celebrated by sailors nationwide.
In many other Russian coastal towns, too, the naval forces celebrated the day of navy with parades.
In Syria, where Russia is fighting alongsite Bashar al-Assad, the military demonstrated strength at an arms show in the Mediterranean port of Tartu's strength.
Ammunition was also fired there.
In a video released by the Ministry of Defence, not only warships, including a submarine, but also combat helicopters and jets were seen.
Hurricane "Hanna" reaches Texas: Help made more difficult due to Corona
The first hurricane of this year's season over the Atlantic has landed in Texas.
Life-threatening flooding, strong winds and heavy rains now threatened parts of the U.S. state, the U.S. climate agency NOAA's National Hurricane Center said at night on Sunday (local time).
Gov. Greg Abbott declared a state of emergency for numerous counties.
The corona pandemic rampant in Texas complicates the work of the aides, Abbott said, according to U.S. media.
Evacuates called for caution
"Every hurricane is a tremendous challenge," Abbott said, according to the Houston Chronicle newspaper.
Looking at the hurricane in Texas, he added: "This challenge is complicated and will be exacerbated, considering that it is sweeping through an area most affected by Covid-19 in the state."
According to John's Hopkins University, around 5000 people have died so far in Texas alone as a result of the corona pandemic.
Abbott urged people who would have to leave their homes to exercise caution.
They shouldn't disregard practices "that we've become accustomed to," such as wearing masks, the governor said, according to CBS.
It is important for everyone to keep their distance.
Therefore, hotel rooms have also been provided in some places.
In addition, it should be tested in emergency shelters for Corona.
Damage was initially reported only from the coastal town of Port Mansfield.
Storm warning withdrawn again
With wind speeds of up to 150 kilometers per hour, "Hanna" had hit the island of Padre Island on land immediately off the Texas coast, according to authorities.
The tropical storm had previously been upgraded to hurricane of the lowest level one.
For the coastal section from Port Mansfield to Sargent, a storm surge warning had initially been issued and later withdrawn.
Authorities anticipated a weakening, while "Hanna" moved further inland and heading towards the north-east of Mexico.
"Douglas" is moving towards Hawaii
In the Pacific, Hurricane "Douglas" was heading towards Hawaii during the night on Sunday with wind speeds of around 150 kilometers per hour.
The Hurricane Center, however, expected it to weaken further.
According to the forecast, it should be on Sunday (local time) near the largest islands in Hawaii - then as a storm just below the hurricane threshold.
Across the Atlantic, NOAA expected an above-average hurricane season this year, according to a forecast from May.
From June to the end of November, up to ten hurricanes are to be expected, of which up to six could become very strong hurricanes, the authority said.
On average, there are six hurricanes per year over the Atlantic, three of which develop into storms of great strength.
In view of available climate data and the higher water temperature in the Atlantic and the Caribbean, the probability of a normal season is only 30 percent, that of a below-average even only ten percent, it was said at the time.
U.S. President Donald Trump tweeted that his administration is closely monitoring the storms and is in contact with the affected states.
Four goals revoked: Video assistant "punished" Sassuolo
Sassuolo's Filip Djuricic were stripped two goals
Naples - Four goals scored, four goals cancelled: Sassuolo Calcio experienced a raven-black evening at 0:2 (0:1) at the SSC Naples in the Italian football league Serie A - and was really angry at the video assistant of the referee.
"A festival of not given goals for Sassuolo.
Never has the VAR acted so harshly against a team," commented the "Corriere dello Sport".
