The Video Assistant Referee (VAR) retracted the hit because of an offside position.
Five minutes later, Djuricic shot the ball into the net again - the VAR intervened again.
The goals from attackers Francesco Caputo (50.) and Domenico Berardi (61.) also took the refereeing team back after hinting from the VAR.
In the aftermath, Allan made the 2:0 final score perfect for Naples and the bitter evening for Sassuolo.
“The VAR is worse for Sassuolo than a radar trap on the road, punishing Sassuolo like no team before.
The new technology sets records," writes Gazzetta dello Sport.
FC Bayern Munich: Pavard injured
Munich (dpa) - The start to the hot preparation phase for the Champions League began for double winners FC Bayern with a setback.
Hansi Flick, who recovered after a short holiday and a few days of training "somewhat well", has to consider how he can rebuild his successful defence after the injury of world champion Benjamin Pavard.
The Frenchman, who has the third-most minutes behind captain Manuel Neuer and midfield boss Joshua Kimmich, suffered a ligament injury to his left root and threatens to be missing in crucial premier class duels.
In Munich's smuggling weather, the right-back broke off a training game and was driven off the course with a golf kart.
How long the unrestricted regular has to pause in the battle for the title is open to question.
It is likely that it would be difficult to play in the quarter-final second leg against Chelsea on 8 August, and the 24-year-old can hope for the final tournament, which begins in Lisbon on 12 August.
Bayern, however, don't want to be stopped by mishappiness from Sunday's training.
Flick, in his personal triple formula, mentioned the three attributes he wants to see from his stars on the way to Portugal's capital.
Quality, intensity and concentration are to show his victory-hungry title collectors in the ideally following 28 days until the premier class finals on 23 August.
“We mastered the mandatory tasks with championship and troph simply excellently.
Now it's time to create what's special," Flick said.
However, until Munich can deal concretely with the final tournament and previously a training camp in the Algarve, the eighth final obligation against Chelsea is due for the first time.
After 3:0 in the first leg before the Corona break, the Londoners in the domestic arena should not become a Bayern nightmare again as in the lost "final dahoam" in 2012.
“We have to go through the door first.
We want to win, create good conditions and gain confidence with a good game," Flick demanded.
A test to tune in will take place against Olympique Marseille on Friday.
With a lengthy address, Flick had sworn his stars, who had 13 days of vacation after the double, on a one-off August.
He welcomed 31 players at Säbener Straße, including the new players around noble shopping Leroy Sané, who met all new colleagues on the pitch for the first time after his move from Manchester City.
"Leroy, like all the newcomers, blended in well," Flick reported.
But instead of talking about the new season, when Sané & Co. are no longer just allowed to co-train, Flick much preferred to talk about the current title project of the German series champion.
After days of individual and small-group training is now being trained in team strength for the Londoners, who, in contrast to the Flick ensemble, are in the rhythm of the game.
After that, Munich could face a quarter-final against FC Barcelona and Lionel Messi, in the semi-finals Real Madrid, Manchester City or Juventus Turin are possible opponents.
A way to the final in Lisbon "isn't quite that easy," insisted the head coach, who can rely on the services of his new assistant Miroslav Klose in training from Tuesday.
The World Cup record scorer Klose and Flick know about so many joint tournament preparation, which is particularly important in the 0-to-100 attunement.
With his current team, working is "a pleasure," Flick praised.
"Focusedness on winning" is an impressive trait of his team.
At the tournament with nine-man teams on Sunday it was also ambitious to the cause.
Of the high achievers, all of the performers were involved, including the churminant Thiago and defender David Alaba, whose whereabouts beyond the premier class season is not assured.
Formula 1 needs a special rule to choose its world champion
Since it is not possible to drive on the required number of continents, the FIA must improvise in order to award the World Cup titles.
However, all American races have been cancelled.
Race calendar is becoming more and more concrete
The racing calendar of the current Formula 1 season continues to take shape.
In addition to the ten confirmed Grand Prix so far, three other racetracks were addeded during the week.
In October at the Nürburgring in Germany and Portimão is to be driven.
On the first of November, Lewis Hamilton and Co. will compete for World Championship points at the traditional Italian circuit in Imola.
According to the current status, the 2020 racing calendar thus comprises 13 runs.
"We are pleased that we will continue to make great progress in completing our plans for the 2020 season," Formula One boss Chase Carey said.
In total, 15 to 18 races are to be completed in the premier class of motorsport in this extraordinary season.
Formula 1 wants to conclude its season in Asia.
The final race calendar is to be fixed and published in the coming weeks.
Ahead of the already officially targeted races in Bahrain and Abu Dhabi in December, according to media reports, appearances in Vietnam and Malaysia could still take place at the end of November.
Regulations demand racing on three continents
The regulations of Formula 1 require, however, at least races on three continents for the awarding of the world championship.
Even if, as currently planned, only two continents is driven, there should be a world champion.
Using a special rule, the FIA intends to award the titles, citing "extraordinary circumstances" because of the coronavirus pandemic.
The traditional start of the season in Australia in March had to be cancelled at short notice due to the imminent corona crisis.
A return to the continent later this year is not an issue.
What's more, Formula 1 won't be a guest in America this year.
Relegation is met with criticism in Brazil
Due to the tense corona situation, the races will not take place in Canada, the USA, Mexico and Brazil.
"We're looking forward to being back with them next season," Carey explained.
The cancellation of the American races met with criticism, especially in Brazil: "The cancellation did not come as a surprise to us, but we cannot accept the reasons for this," said Tamas Rohonyi, promoter of the São Paulo Grand Prix.
Thus, the number of infections in the São Paulo area is significantly lower than in other regions of Brazil.
Moreover, the concerns of some teams about not wanting to drive in Brazil would not justify a rejection.
Be prepared for the required security measures.
Greta Thunberg: Bad look against Donald Trump went around the world
Greta Thunberg talked about her encounter with U.S. President Donald Trump.
Greta Thunberg was a videon in the well-known Latentight show of Stephen Colbert and revealed in the interview what she had in mind during her encounter with Donald Trump.
Climate activist Greta Thunberg was a guest on a popular U.S. latenight show via video.
Among other things, she told about her fight against the world's powerful.
Also talking about her famous "evil gaze" at her encounter with Donald Trump, the teenage girl.
New York City - Over the past two years, climate activist Greta Thunberg drew attention with her tireless commitment to the "Fridays For Future" movement.
Now she spoke in a well-known Latenight show about her plans in the future as well as her encounter with U.S. President Donald Trump at last year's U.N. climate summit.
Latenight presenter Stephen Colbert greeted 17-year-old Thunberg in your broadcast on Tuesday and conducted a lengthy interview with the Swede.
In doing so, she talked not only about her fight against climate change, but also about her conflict with the powerful of the world.
"There are a few things that surprised me," she said.
"How far people are going to turn the focus away from the climate crisis," she said.
"Everything from organized hate campaigns to threats just because a group of children is taking to the streets," she said.
Heads of state and leaders who scoff against them on social media or even try to silence them find them "funny," as the reactions show her "as they have no other answer to it."
It became particularly interesting at the end of the interview, when Greta Thunberg was approached about a well-known scene in which she threw U.S. President Trump a bitterly evil look.
At the U.N. climate summit in New York in 2019, for which the nude activist sailed across the Atlantic, her expressive gaze was captured by the cameras and has since been seen by countless people on the internet.
From the short clip, a GIF emerged, i.e. an animated image, which ultimately became one of the most commonly used images for Trump opponents.
But what did the 17-year-old think at that moment about the US president?
In response to Stephen Colbert's relatively open-minded question, she replied only briefly dry: "To be honest, I don't think that needs any further explanation."
The experienced satirist Colbert probably did not expect the quick-witted answer and disappeared from the picture.
With her "How dare you?" speech on the principles of climate protection, she was reminded by many at the climate conference.
Ex-Dortmunder Immobile with three-pack for Lazio Rome
Ciro Immobile is now on par with Bayern's Robert Lewandowski in the race for Europe's Golden Shoe.
Former Dortmund international Ciro Immobile continues to have best chances to become top scorer in the Italian football championship.
The one-time BVB professional contributed three goals in the 5:1 (1:1) victory of Lazio Rome at Hellas Verona, including two converted penalty kicks in the post-match period of both halves.
