In Italy, Cristiano Ronaldo follows with 30 goals so far, the Portuguese superstar met Sampdoria Genoa in the evening with the front-runner.
Fleetwood-Mac co-founder Peter Green dies
His life was like a rollercoaster ride: after a rapid ascent with Fleetwood Mac it went steeply downhill and then slowly upwards again.
Blues rock guitarist Peter Green has died at the age of 73.
Ingenious guitarist, drug junkie, cult supporter: The co-founder of Fleetwood Mac, Peter Green, has died.
He had died peacefully in his sleep at the age of 73, family members said in an official statement on Saturday, a further opinion would be announced for the next few days.
Top musicians like Peter Frampton paid tribute to Green as one of the best blues and rock guitarists.
Mick Fleetwood spoke in an emotional response of a "monumental loss" for all previous and current members of the band Fleetwood Mac.
The drummer Fleetwood and the guitarist Green had founded the meanwhile legendary band in 1967 in London, to this day unforgotten is his dreamy instrumental hit "Albatross".
"The musician's life ("The End Of The Game", "In The Skies") resembled a rollercoaster ride.
With Fleetwood Mac, Green experienced a rapid rise - the band celebrated its greatest success but then without him.
Among other things, there is said to have been dispute because Green wanted to donate all of the group's profits to charity.
He began to perform in wide robes and with great crucifix.
Mental health problems are also said to have played a role.
Just three years after its founding, Green left the band in 1970.
This was followed by a social descent.
The blues rock guitarist suffered from substance abuse and schizophrenia, biographers reported.
For a long time Green was treated in psychiatry.
During much of the 1970s, the musician disappeared from the scene.
He is said to have lived temporarily on the street, worked as a cemetery gardener and spent some time in an Israeli kibbutz.
Green was born in 1946 as the son of a Jewish working-class family in Bethnal Green.
From an early age, he became enthusiastic about US blues musicians such as B.B. King and Muddy Waters.
At the age of eleven, he learned the first guitar handles.
At the age of 15, the teenager started playing guitar professionally and five years later got the chance to be on stage as a lead guitarist for the instrumental band Peter B's Looners.
A little later he was hired as a replacement for his idol Eric Clapton with the Bluesbreakers.
In the middle of the blues boom, Green founded Fleetwood Mac in the summer of 1967 along with his Bluesbreakers colleagues Mick Fleetwood and John McVie.
The song "Albatross", composed by Green, gave the group the first number 1 hit in the winter of 1968/69 and catapulted it to the top of the British music scene.
But the rapid ascent soon showed his downsides: Green couldn't cope with the media buzz, his experiments with the drug LSD became increasingly extreme.
The life between music and addiction, he later commented in an interview: "The worst thing that has happened to me in life is the drugs - the best thing was to land a number 1 hit".
Towards the end of the decade, Green surprisingly achieved a respectable success in 1979 with an album inspired by religious themes.
Fleetwood Mac, meanwhile, swam on a wave of success without him in a new formation.
Their 1977 album "Rumours" sold more than 40 million copies worldwide.
Despite various small solo successes, Green did not make a major comeback until he started in 1996 with a group of friendly musicians.
Britain's "Daily Telegraph" at the time spoke of a rock star who had returned to earth.
Free from drugs, Green released several albums with the Peter Green Splinter Group until the band dissolved in 2004.
For music experts, he was already a blues legend during his lifetime - and with his gentle sound one of the most influential guitarists of the past decades.
